The code statements that lead to the bug are:
list_add(&buf->list, &bl->buf_list);
bl->nbufs++;
Reason:
These lines unconditionally add the buffer back to the list and increment the count without checking if the buffer list is still valid or if it has been upgraded to a ring-based type. This allows a buffer from a legacy list to be added to a ring-based list or a non-existent list, causing corruption.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (bl && !(bl->flags & IOBL_BUF_RING)) {
    list_add(&buf->list, &bl->buf_list);
    bl->nbufs++;
} else {
    kfree(buf);
}
req->kbuf = NULL;
Reason:
The condition ensures the buffer is only recycled if the list exists and is still a legacy list. Otherwise, the buffer is freed to prevent misuse. Setting `req->kbuf = NULL` explicitly clears the reference, avoiding stale pointer usage.

